WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:06.910
{\an8}<i> In the criminal justice system.</i>

2
00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:08.883
{\an8}<i>the people are represented</i>

3
00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:11.302
{\an8}<i>by two separate.
yet equally important groups:</i>

4
00:00:11.428 --> 00:00:13.263
{\an8}<i>the police.
who investigate crime.</i>

5
00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:15.974
{\an8}<i>and the district attorneys.
who prosecute the offenders.</i>

6
00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:17.809
{\an8}<i>These are their stories.</i>

7
00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:23.064
I heard the good news.

8
00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:24.649
I still can't believe it.

9
00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:26.818
And thank you so much
for writing that letter.

10
00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:28.403
It means the world to me.

11
00:00:28.486 --> 00:00:29.446
He's a great kid.

12
00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.114
Princeton's lucky to get him.

13
00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:32.407
No. I mean that.

14
00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:44.127
Really?

15
00:00:44.294 --> 00:00:46.296
He doesn't even nod hello?

16
00:00:46.421 --> 00:00:48.382
I mean. here we are. working
on a Saturday to make him rich.

17
00:00:48.465 --> 00:00:49.549
He doesn't even say hello?

18
00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:50.800
You're missing the point.

19
00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:52.511
We're here to make
ourselves rich.

20
00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:54.929
We're using him.
just like he's using us.

21
00:00:57.932 --> 00:00:59.059
The lady over there
saw the body.

22
00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:00.685
called 911.

23
00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:03.438
-Did she see anything else?
-No. sir. Just the body.

24
00:01:03.521 --> 00:01:04.898
Any other potential witnesses?

25
00:01:04.981 --> 00:01:06.191
No. sir.

26
00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.445
That's a lot of rage.

27
00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.365
Those are some very deep wounds.

28
00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.076
Thinking maybe a pipe
or maybe a bat?

29
00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:17.452
Got an ID?

30
00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:20.538
We didn't touch him.
We were waiting for you guys.

31
00:01:20.663 --> 00:01:23.125
His wedding band
and watch are still on him.

32
00:01:23.208 --> 00:01:24.584
Got a wallet.

33
00:01:26.836 --> 00:01:30.006
Jon Geller. Higson Capital.

34
00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.049
Geller.

35
00:01:31.132 --> 00:01:32.843
I know that name.

36
00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:33.968
Wall Street guy. right?

37
00:01:34.052 --> 00:01:35.971
He's kind of a legend.

38
00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:37.139
Can you bag that?

39
00:01:37.222 --> 00:01:38.390
Yes. ma'am.

40
00:01:39.724 --> 00:01:41.184
Probably on his way
to his beach house

41
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:44.104
or someplace fabulous.

42
00:01:44.187 --> 00:01:45.063
Yeah. but once in a while.

43
00:01:45.146 --> 00:01:47.190
it's not so good to be a legend.

44
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.535
{\an8}Cause of death was...
you guessed it...

45
00:02:40.618 --> 00:02:41.911
{\an8}blunt force trauma.

46
00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:43.663
{\an8}Pretty vicious attack.

47
00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.875
{\an8}10. possibly 11. blows
to the head and face

48
00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:48.085
{\an8}with a heavy object.

49
00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:49.627
{\an8}Any idea what kind?

50
00:02:49.711 --> 00:02:52.130
{\an8}No. but based on the wounds.
something with a sharp

51
00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:55.342
{\an8}and thin edge to it.
like a crowbar.

52
00:02:55.467 --> 00:02:56.384
{\an8}Anything else?

53
00:02:56.468 --> 00:02:57.927
{\an8}Found some third-party DNA

54
00:02:58.011 --> 00:02:59.513
{\an8}under Geller's nails.

55
00:02:59.596 --> 00:03:00.806
{\an8}Figures.

56
00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:02.266
{\an8}The guy's been
scratching and clawing

57
00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:03.892
{\an8}his entire life. right?

58
00:03:07.395 --> 00:03:08.855
{\an8}Are you sure?

59
00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.566
{\an8}He's not the only Jon Geller
in New York.

60
00:03:11.649 --> 00:03:13.735
{\an8}Yeah. we're sure.

61
00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.987
{\an8}Um. I'm very sorry.

62
00:03:16.112 --> 00:03:18.031
{\an8}We just need to ask you
a few questions.

63
00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:20.492
{\an8}Were you aware of his plans
for the day?

64
00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:22.535
{\an8}Uh. he was heading
out to Long Island

65
00:03:22.619 --> 00:03:24.162
{\an8}to play golf with a client.

66
00:03:24.245 --> 00:03:25.247
{\an8}Was he driving?

67
00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:26.498
{\an8}No. no. no.

68
00:03:26.623 --> 00:03:29.292
{\an8}He was... he was taking
his helicopter.

69
00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:32.337
{\an8}He stopped by the office first.

70
00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.506
{\an8}Was there anything unusual
happening lately?

71
00:03:34.589 --> 00:03:38.426
{\an8}Anyone harassing him.
stalking him. threatening him?

72
00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:42.305
{\an8}-Oh.
-Whoa. whoa. whoa.

73
00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:43.765
{\an8}I got you. OK. all right.

74
00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:44.891
{\an8}Take a seat. Take a seat.

75
00:03:44.974 --> 00:03:45.767
{\an8}OK. breathe.

76
00:03:45.934 --> 00:03:47.185
{\an8}OK. just breathe.

77
00:03:52.816 --> 00:03:53.859
{\an8}<i> According to
the video surveillance</i>

78
00:03:53.942 --> 00:03:55.319
{\an8}we just watched.
you guys met with Geller

79
00:03:55.402 --> 00:03:56.569
{\an8}earlier this morning?

80
00:03:56.695 --> 00:03:58.864
{\an8}Uh. correct.

81
00:03:58.947 --> 00:04:01.282
{\an8}And how long
did you guys meet for?

82
00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:03.660
{\an8}About ten minutes. maybe less.

83
00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:05.120
{\an8}What'd you talk about?

84
00:04:05.203 --> 00:04:07.038
{\an8}Business.

85
00:04:07.122 --> 00:04:11.418
{\an8}Did he seem upset or agitated
or everything seemed normal?

86
00:04:11.543 --> 00:04:13.128
{\an8}Uh. both.

87
00:04:13.211 --> 00:04:14.754
{\an8}What does that mean?

88
00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:16.632
{\an8}Jon's normal state of being...
at work. anyway...

89
00:04:16.715 --> 00:04:20.343
{\an8}was being agitated. so both.

90
00:04:20.468 --> 00:04:21.511
{\an8}That your impression as well?

91
00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:23.847
{\an8}Yeah. more or less.

92
00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:26.266
{\an8}He was a little more agitated
than usual.

93
00:04:26.391 --> 00:04:27.934
{\an8}Why is that?

94
00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:30.687
{\an8}Because the deal we were
working on hasn't closed yet.

95
00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:33.732
{\an8}He thought we weren't
working hard enough.

96
00:04:33.815 --> 00:04:36.192
{\an8}Did he mention anything
about his plans for the day.

97
00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:37.945
{\an8}where he was going.
who he was meeting with?

98
00:04:38.028 --> 00:04:39.946
{\an8}No.

99
00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.350
{\an8}To your knowledge. was anyone
harassing or threatening him?

100
00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:43.742
{\an8}No.

101
00:04:43.825 --> 00:04:45.118
{\an8}Is there anything
you can tell us

102
00:04:45.201 --> 00:04:46.995
{\an8}that you think might be helpful?

103
00:04:49.205 --> 00:04:51.791
{\an8}He was carrying a putter.

104
00:04:51.875 --> 00:04:53.668
{\an8}He just had it custom made.

105
00:04:53.752 --> 00:04:56.421
{\an8}He said it was going
to change his life.

106
00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.843
{\an8}Good chance that new putter
was the murder weapon.

107
00:05:01.926 --> 00:05:04.095
{\an8}Really changed
the guy's life too.

108
00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:05.597
{\an8}Excuse me.

109
00:05:05.722 --> 00:05:07.683
{\an8}Are you the assistant
for Mr. Geller?

110
00:05:07.766 --> 00:05:10.268
{\an8}Yes. I heard what happened.
and I rushed over.

111
00:05:10.393 --> 00:05:12.145
{\an8}I wanted to see
if I could help with anything.

112
00:05:12.228 --> 00:05:13.688
{\an8}What time did you arrive?

113
00:05:13.772 --> 00:05:15.732
{\an8}About ten minutes ago.

114
00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:17.776
{\an8}Did you happen to talk with

115
00:05:17.942 --> 00:05:19.569
{\an8}or text Mr. Geller this morning?

116
00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:22.322
{\an8}Yes. we texted around 8:30.

117
00:05:22.447 --> 00:05:23.365
{\an8}About what?

118
00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.700
{\an8}His flight to Bridgehampton.

119
00:05:25.825 --> 00:05:28.411
{\an8}Was he traveling alone?

120
00:05:28.495 --> 00:05:29.662
{\an8}No.

121
00:05:32.123 --> 00:05:34.084
{\an8}I was waiting for him
at the helipad.

122
00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:35.502
{\an8}I just thought
he was running late

123
00:05:35.585 --> 00:05:38.213
{\an8}or got caught
on an early morning Zoom.

124
00:05:38.296 --> 00:05:40.965
{\an8}So I left and got a coffee.

125
00:05:41.049 --> 00:05:42.551
{\an8}And you and Mr. Geller were
heading to Long Island

126
00:05:42.634 --> 00:05:43.719
{\an8}to play golf?

127
00:05:43.802 --> 00:05:45.178
{\an8}Is that right?

128
00:05:45.261 --> 00:05:46.888
{\an8}Yes.

129
00:05:46.971 --> 00:05:48.181
{\an8}Which course?

130
00:05:48.306 --> 00:05:50.726
{\an8}Um. the one he belongs to.

131
00:05:50.809 --> 00:05:51.810
{\an8}It's private.

132
00:05:51.893 --> 00:05:53.186
{\an8}You don't know the name of it?

133
00:05:53.269 --> 00:05:54.604
{\an8}No.

134
00:05:54.688 --> 00:05:55.897
{\an8}Let me remind you
that we're investigating

135
00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:57.482
{\an8}a homicide here. OK?

136
00:05:57.565 --> 00:05:59.192
{\an8}So anything that you say
is actually...

137
00:05:59.275 --> 00:06:01.069
Sorry.

138
00:06:01.152 --> 00:06:03.697
We weren't going out
to play golf.

139
00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:06.074
We were gonna spend
the weekend together.

140
00:06:06.157 --> 00:06:07.992
Just the two of you?

141
00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:09.744
We were dating.

142
00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:16.292
So was this
a serious thing or...

143
00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:17.628
Yes.

144
00:06:17.711 --> 00:06:20.964
Did Mr. Geller's wife know?

145
00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:23.508
Jon said he was gonna
tell her about us.

146
00:06:23.591 --> 00:06:25.469
ask for a divorce.

147
00:06:25.552 --> 00:06:27.053
But he didn't wanna hurt her.

148
00:06:27.178 --> 00:06:29.139
Did you actually see Jon
this morning?

149
00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:30.974
Speak to him?
-No.

150
00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:32.768
He told me to meet him
at the helipad at 10:00.

151
00:06:32.851 --> 00:06:36.312
Any altercations? Any arguments?

152
00:06:36.396 --> 00:06:37.856
Well. last night.
Jon and his wife

153
00:06:37.939 --> 00:06:39.941
got into an argument
over the phone.

154
00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:41.652
It sounded pretty heated.

155
00:06:41.735 --> 00:06:43.403
You know what it was about?

156
00:06:43.528 --> 00:06:45.072
Not really.

157
00:06:45.155 --> 00:06:47.741
But I heard Jon say.
this is happening.

158
00:06:47.824 --> 00:06:49.492
whether you like it or not.

159
00:06:49.576 --> 00:06:51.995
And then he just sort of
stormed off.

160
00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:54.247
told me he'd meet me
at the helipad.

161
00:06:54.372 --> 00:06:55.623
And that was it.

162
00:06:55.707 --> 00:06:57.960
That was the last time
I saw him.

163
00:06:58.043 --> 00:07:00.337
Do you think it's possible
that Geller told her

164
00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:03.048
about you and the divorce?

165
00:07:03.131 --> 00:07:04.174
Yeah.

166
00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:07.093
He said he was going to.

167
00:07:07.218 --> 00:07:08.845
Wait. you don't think that...

168
00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:21.459
Wait. you're telling me
that Jon was...

169
00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:22.900
he was dating a man?

170
00:07:22.984 --> 00:07:23.902
Yeah.

171
00:07:24.027 --> 00:07:26.363
His name is Matt Thomas.

172
00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:27.906
I find that
very hard to believe.

173
00:07:27.989 --> 00:07:29.283
We're just telling you
what we know.

174
00:07:29.366 --> 00:07:30.700
This man was meeting Jon

175
00:07:30.784 --> 00:07:32.411
at the East End helipad
yesterday morning.

176
00:07:32.494 --> 00:07:35.747
And he also says he heard
Jon having an argument with you

177
00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.541
the night before he was killed.

178
00:07:37.624 --> 00:07:38.875
What are you talking about?

179
00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.460
They were out at a bar.

180
00:07:40.543 --> 00:07:42.337
and he heard Jon say.
this is happening.

181
00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.090
whether you like it or not.

182
00:07:45.215 --> 00:07:48.009
Was he asking you for a divorce?

183
00:07:48.093 --> 00:07:49.135
A divorce?

184
00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:50.929
No.

185
00:07:51.054 --> 00:07:54.140
We were arguing
about our son. Shane.

186
00:07:54.224 --> 00:07:56.017
Jon wanted him
to repeat the sixth grade.

187
00:07:56.142 --> 00:07:58.478
He thought it would give him
a tactical advantage.

188
00:07:58.561 --> 00:08:00.731
you know. him being older
than other kids.

189
00:08:00.814 --> 00:08:02.857
And I thought it was overkill.

190
00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:06.194
That's what we were
arguing about.

191
00:08:06.319 --> 00:08:08.947
Did you find the person
who killed my dad?

192
00:08:11.074 --> 00:08:12.743
No. not yet. but we are
doing everything we can.

193
00:08:12.826 --> 00:08:14.160
I promise you.

194
00:08:14.244 --> 00:08:16.621
Shane. honey.
please go up to your room. OK?

195
00:08:18.873 --> 00:08:21.042
Mrs. Geller.
I apologize in advance.

196
00:08:21.126 --> 00:08:25.338
but where were you
yesterday morning at 9:45 a.m.?

197
00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:29.509
Well. I had a Pilates class
in the West Village.

198
00:08:29.634 --> 00:08:31.678
I know
we asked you this earlier.

199
00:08:31.761 --> 00:08:33.680
but can you think of anyone
that was harassing

200
00:08:33.805 --> 00:08:35.348
or threatening your husband?

201
00:08:38.393 --> 00:08:40.061
There's this one guy at work.

202
00:08:40.145 --> 00:08:43.398
He was threatening to sue Jon
for a lot of money.

203
00:08:45.316 --> 00:08:46.985
You all right?

204
00:08:47.068 --> 00:08:48.069
Yeah.

205
00:08:49.988 --> 00:08:52.949
I was thinking
about Geller's wife.

206
00:08:53.074 --> 00:08:54.951
Dead bodies I can handle.

207
00:08:55.035 --> 00:08:58.079
but telling innocent people
secrets about their loved ones?

208
00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:00.290
Not my favorite.

209
00:09:00.415 --> 00:09:02.167
Yeah. I get it.

210
00:09:02.292 --> 00:09:04.836
Part of the deal. though. right?

211
00:09:04.961 --> 00:09:07.505
Talking with perfect strangers
on the worst day of their life.

212
00:09:07.589 --> 00:09:10.383
asking them personal questions.
telling them things

213
00:09:10.467 --> 00:09:13.094
they never knew
about their loved ones.

214
00:09:13.219 --> 00:09:14.763
Hell of a job.

215
00:09:18.683 --> 00:09:21.478
Sorry to bother you. Mr. Addis.

216
00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:23.104
I'm sort of
in the middle of something.

217
00:09:23.188 --> 00:09:24.939
Eh. this won't take long.

218
00:09:25.023 --> 00:09:27.359
We want to know why you were
threatening to sue Geller.

219
00:09:27.484 --> 00:09:29.819
Excuse me.

220
00:09:29.903 --> 00:09:31.405
Jon led me to believe
that I was going to be

221
00:09:31.488 --> 00:09:33.365
named CEO when he retired.

222
00:09:33.448 --> 00:09:36.576
Told me that. like.
five or six times.

223
00:09:36.701 --> 00:09:38.453
But he appointed
somebody else instead?

224
00:09:38.536 --> 00:09:39.829
Carter Getzelman.

225
00:09:39.954 --> 00:09:41.164
Good guy.

226
00:09:41.247 --> 00:09:42.499
We went to business school
together.

227
00:09:42.582 --> 00:09:44.292
But I was still upset.

228
00:09:44.376 --> 00:09:46.962
I've had lots of lucrative
offers over the past few years.

229
00:09:47.045 --> 00:09:49.798
and every time I got one.
Jon persuaded me to stay.

230
00:09:49.923 --> 00:09:52.342
told me I was going to run
the firm when he retired.

231
00:09:52.425 --> 00:09:53.968
But I never actually sued him.

232
00:09:54.052 --> 00:09:55.261
Why is that?

233
00:09:55.387 --> 00:09:57.389
We talked it out
and renegotiated

234
00:09:57.472 --> 00:09:59.474
my employment contract
for the better.

235
00:09:59.557 --> 00:10:01.143
How come you didn't tell us this

236
00:10:01.226 --> 00:10:02.185
last time we spoke?

237
00:10:02.268 --> 00:10:04.021
Well. you never asked.

238
00:10:04.104 --> 00:10:06.481
I'm pretty sure we did.

239
00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:08.316
Huh.

240
00:10:08.441 --> 00:10:09.943
I lied. Habit.

241
00:10:10.068 --> 00:10:11.194
Excuse me?

242
00:10:11.277 --> 00:10:13.238
Three rules of Wall Street...

243
00:10:13.321 --> 00:10:16.199
never admit defeat.
never use your own money.

244
00:10:16.282 --> 00:10:18.201
and never tell the truth.

245
00:10:18.326 --> 00:10:19.911
-Are you trying to be funny?
-Mm. a little.

246
00:10:19.994 --> 00:10:21.329
Knock it off.

247
00:10:23.248 --> 00:10:25.750
I didn't kill Jon Geller.

248
00:10:25.875 --> 00:10:27.460
That's ridiculous.

249
00:10:27.544 --> 00:10:30.463
Truth is. I liked him
and respected him.

250
00:10:30.547 --> 00:10:33.550
He taught me a lot
and made me rich too.

251
00:10:33.633 --> 00:10:35.135
You happen to exit
the building that day

252
00:10:35.218 --> 00:10:36.761
after Geller left?

253
00:10:36.845 --> 00:10:38.847
I went for a quick lunch
around 1:00.

254
00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:39.764
Got a receipt?

255
00:10:39.848 --> 00:10:41.099
Paid in cash.

256
00:10:41.182 --> 00:10:42.582
Didn't know
you could still do that.

257
00:10:43.810 --> 00:10:44.810
Ooh.

258
00:10:44.894 --> 00:10:47.188
Guys. I need to take this.

259
00:10:48.982 --> 00:10:50.358
Addis.

260
00:11:00.493 --> 00:11:03.288
These Wall Street dudes are
so proud of being ruthless.

261
00:11:03.371 --> 00:11:05.874
I'm not sure if this
Addis guy is as OK

262
00:11:05.999 --> 00:11:07.667
as he suggests
at being passed over.

263
00:11:07.792 --> 00:11:09.336
Only thing
more intoxicating than money

264
00:11:09.419 --> 00:11:11.046
is power. right?
-Yeah.

265
00:11:11.129 --> 00:11:12.881
And how about that video?

266
00:11:13.006 --> 00:11:14.258
There's footage of him
leaving the building

267
00:11:14.341 --> 00:11:16.092
at 12:49 p.m.

268
00:11:16.176 --> 00:11:17.427
Nothing before that?

269
00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:19.096
No. but I'm guessing
there's a back exit.

270
00:11:19.179 --> 00:11:21.499
For all we know. he could have
snuck out and snuck back in.

271
00:11:22.474 --> 00:11:24.267
DNA results are in.

272
00:11:35.862 --> 00:11:37.280
OK.

273
00:11:37.405 --> 00:11:39.616
This is just officially
getting ridiculous.

274
00:11:39.699 --> 00:11:41.034
Yes. it is. but we need to talk.

275
00:11:41.117 --> 00:11:42.535
And why is that?

276
00:11:42.619 --> 00:11:43.912
Because your DNA matches
the DNA found

277
00:11:43.995 --> 00:11:45.246
under Geller's fingernails.

278
00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:46.247
That's impossible.

279
00:11:46.331 --> 00:11:47.749
No. it's not impossible at all.

280
00:11:47.874 --> 00:11:49.126
We borrowed
some of the coffee cups

281
00:11:49.209 --> 00:11:50.127
from the conference room.

282
00:11:50.210 --> 00:11:51.127
Turns out the saliva...

283
00:11:51.252 --> 00:11:52.879
I don't drink coffee.

284
00:11:53.004 --> 00:11:54.381
Never have.

285
00:11:54.506 --> 00:11:56.925
So if the cups you borrowed
had coffee in them.

286
00:11:57.050 --> 00:11:58.343
it wasn't me.

287
00:11:58.426 --> 00:12:00.470
This is my stimulant of choice.

288
00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:06.559
Nick Rossi.

289
00:12:06.643 --> 00:12:07.852
What's going on?

290
00:12:07.977 --> 00:12:09.104
Can you give me that cup

291
00:12:09.229 --> 00:12:10.314
and put your hands
behind your back?

292
00:12:10.397 --> 00:12:11.481
Why?

293
00:12:11.564 --> 00:12:12.649
Because you're under arrest

294
00:12:12.732 --> 00:12:14.025
for the murder of Jon Geller.

295
00:12:14.109 --> 00:12:15.444
What?

296
00:12:15.527 --> 00:12:17.070
We found your DNA under
Jon Geller's fingernails.

297
00:12:17.153 --> 00:12:18.697
People who commit murder
shouldn't leave

298
00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:20.490
half-empty coffee cups
lying around.

299
00:12:28.373 --> 00:12:31.251
We just found your DNA
under Geller's fingernails.

300
00:12:32.919 --> 00:12:35.172
And a patrol officer
just found the putter.

301
00:12:35.255 --> 00:12:38.467
the one that you used to smash
Geller's skull. in a dumpster.

302
00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:39.760
So I'm guessing we're gonna
find your fingerprints

303
00:12:39.843 --> 00:12:42.303
all over that too.

304
00:12:42.429 --> 00:12:44.764
And if that wasn't enough.
we've got video

305
00:12:44.889 --> 00:12:47.267
of you leaving the office
the morning of the murder

306
00:12:47.392 --> 00:12:50.854
at 8:48 a.m.

307
00:12:50.937 --> 00:12:53.982
You don't return until 11:17.

308
00:12:54.107 --> 00:12:55.775
So what happened. huh?

309
00:12:57.527 --> 00:12:59.654
Why'd you attack this guy?

310
00:13:01.114 --> 00:13:03.533
You know. I looked at your bio.

311
00:13:03.616 --> 00:13:05.535
You graduated
top of your class at Fordham.

312
00:13:05.618 --> 00:13:07.329
You played lacrosse.
You got a clean record.

313
00:13:07.412 --> 00:13:10.832
I mean.
you seem like a good kid.

314
00:13:10.915 --> 00:13:14.127
But good kids don't kill people.

315
00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:16.338
Not without a reason. right?

316
00:13:16.421 --> 00:13:18.173
A good reason?

317
00:13:19.966 --> 00:13:21.760
You know. Nick.
believe it or not.

318
00:13:21.843 --> 00:13:23.845
I actually am trying
to help you here.

319
00:13:23.928 --> 00:13:25.555
but I can't do that

320
00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:28.391
if you don't tell me
your side of the story.

321
00:13:28.516 --> 00:13:30.643
So just start at the beginning.

322
00:13:30.727 --> 00:13:31.895
What did Geller say?
What did Geller do?

323
00:13:31.978 --> 00:13:33.688
He is the most disrespectful
human being

324
00:13:33.813 --> 00:13:35.106
I've ever met.

325
00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:37.234
He treats people like...
he acts like...

326
00:13:37.317 --> 00:13:39.694
he acts like he owns you.

327
00:13:41.363 --> 00:13:44.366
That must be
very hard to deal with.

328
00:13:44.491 --> 00:13:46.034
I know if someone treated me
that way day after day...

329
00:13:46.117 --> 00:13:47.702
I'm done talking.

330
00:13:47.827 --> 00:13:49.996
I want a lawyer.

331
00:13:55.001 --> 00:13:56.961
Thanks for doing this.

332
00:13:57.045 --> 00:13:58.213
Please.

333
00:14:02.759 --> 00:14:05.303
The evidence against
Mr. Rossi is strong.

334
00:14:05.387 --> 00:14:08.306
It's clear
that he killed Mr. Geller.

335
00:14:08.390 --> 00:14:12.185
What I'm trying
to understand is why.

336
00:14:12.268 --> 00:14:14.270
Was there anything going on
between these two?

337
00:14:14.396 --> 00:14:16.189
Anything unusual?

338
00:14:16.314 --> 00:14:18.609
Anything unrelated to work?

339
00:14:18.692 --> 00:14:20.902
Not that I'm aware of.

340
00:14:20.985 --> 00:14:22.571
But they definitely weren't
romantically involved.

341
00:14:22.654 --> 00:14:24.322
if that's what
you're suggesting.

342
00:14:24.406 --> 00:14:26.825
And what made Rossi.
who is seemingly

343
00:14:26.908 --> 00:14:29.661
a normal. law-abiding young man.

344
00:14:29.786 --> 00:14:31.913
commit such a violent act?

345
00:14:35.917 --> 00:14:39.171
{\an8}The defendant worked
with Jon Geller for five years.

346
00:14:39.254 --> 00:14:42.382
He was paid
quite handsomely as well.

347
00:14:42.465 --> 00:14:48.054
Last year. his total
compensation was $745.000.

348
00:14:48.138 --> 00:14:51.141
That's not bad for someone
who just turned 27.

349
00:14:51.266 --> 00:14:55.729
And yet. the defendant
despised John Geller.

350
00:14:55.854 --> 00:14:58.273
He thought he was disrespectful.

351
00:14:58.356 --> 00:15:00.025
dismissive. abusive.

352
00:15:00.108 --> 00:15:02.152
And for the most part.
he'd been able

353
00:15:02.235 --> 00:15:04.863
to keep this disdain in check

354
00:15:04.946 --> 00:15:07.824
and carry out
his professional obligations.

355
00:15:07.949 --> 00:15:10.952
But on July 19th.

356
00:15:11.036 --> 00:15:13.830
on a crisp. sunny
Saturday morning.

357
00:15:13.955 --> 00:15:16.583
something triggered him

358
00:15:16.708 --> 00:15:20.712
to viciously attack
and kill Jon Geller.

359
00:15:20.837 --> 00:15:24.549
To be honest. we don't know
the reason for this attack.

360
00:15:24.632 --> 00:15:26.634
but we do know
the evidence will show

361
00:15:26.718 --> 00:15:28.511
the defendant confronted
Mr. Geller

362
00:15:28.636 --> 00:15:30.347
near the East End helipad

363
00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:33.683
and beat him to death

364
00:15:33.808 --> 00:15:35.894
with a golf club.

365
00:15:36.019 --> 00:15:38.730
The defendant may look
like just another

366
00:15:38.855 --> 00:15:42.442
clean-cut former lacrosse player

367
00:15:42.525 --> 00:15:44.027
trying to make it big
on Wall Street.

368
00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:46.446
And for the most part. he was.

369
00:15:46.529 --> 00:15:49.199
until the morning of July 19th. anyway.

370
00:15:49.282 --> 00:15:53.745
On that day. he turned

371
00:15:53.828 --> 00:15:58.917
into a vengeful
and cold-blooded killer.

372
00:16:04.506 --> 00:16:08.426
You've all heard
of Battered Wife Syndrome...

373
00:16:08.551 --> 00:16:11.888
or Abused Person Syndrome.

374
00:16:11.971 --> 00:16:14.849
as they say here in New York.

375
00:16:14.974 --> 00:16:20.397
Nick Rossi was a victim
of something very similar.

376
00:16:20.522 --> 00:16:22.691
Battered Banker Syndrome.

377
00:16:22.774 --> 00:16:24.275
The victims of this abuse suffer

378
00:16:24.359 --> 00:16:27.654
the same debilitating
and disturbing

379
00:16:27.737 --> 00:16:30.323
psychological trauma
as those who suffer

380
00:16:30.448 --> 00:16:32.033
from Abused Person Syndrome.

381
00:16:32.117 --> 00:16:35.537
Jon Geller mentally tortured

382
00:16:35.620 --> 00:16:38.540
and abused Nick Rossi for years.

383
00:16:38.665 --> 00:16:42.586
The abuse was so extreme
and its effect on Mr. Rossi

384
00:16:42.669 --> 00:16:45.547
so profound
that Mr. Rossi truly believed

385
00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:47.757
the only way
to extricate himself

386
00:16:47.841 --> 00:16:50.552
from this abusive relationship

387
00:16:50.635 --> 00:16:53.388
was to kill the man abusing him.

388
00:16:57.726 --> 00:17:01.271
{\an8}"Battered Banker Syndrome"
is neither a legal concept

389
00:17:01.354 --> 00:17:02.939
nor a psychological condition.

390
00:17:03.064 --> 00:17:06.192
It is a made-up buzzword.

391
00:17:06.276 --> 00:17:09.112
It's a clever phrase
the defense invented

392
00:17:09.237 --> 00:17:11.781
with the goal of blaming
Mr. Geller for his own death.

393
00:17:11.906 --> 00:17:13.700
They're... they're trying
to put Geller on trial

394
00:17:13.783 --> 00:17:15.202
instead of the person
who killed him.

395
00:17:15.285 --> 00:17:17.162
This is nothing more
than a thinly veiled attempt

396
00:17:17.245 --> 00:17:19.622
to goad the jury
into ignoring the law.

397
00:17:19.748 --> 00:17:22.376
There's nothing veiled about
what we're trying to do here.

398
00:17:22.459 --> 00:17:26.505
We want people to understand
just how degrading

399
00:17:26.588 --> 00:17:29.924
and stressful it was
to work for Jon Geller.

400
00:17:30.008 --> 00:17:33.095
And this Battered Banker thing?

401
00:17:33.178 --> 00:17:35.555
Is merely a variation
of a well-known condition.

402
00:17:35.638 --> 00:17:37.307
In many ways.
it's the exact same thing.

403
00:17:37.432 --> 00:17:40.477
The psychological consequences
of an abusive employer

404
00:17:40.602 --> 00:17:42.813
to an employee are akin

405
00:17:42.896 --> 00:17:44.939
to those of an abusive husband
to his wife.

406
00:17:45.065 --> 00:17:46.441
Except for the fact that bosses

407
00:17:46.524 --> 00:17:48.443
aren't named in the statute.

408
00:17:48.568 --> 00:17:50.320
This law is meant
to address children.

409
00:17:50.445 --> 00:17:51.738
wives. domestic partners.

410
00:17:51.863 --> 00:17:53.114
Or persons who are or have been

411
00:17:53.198 --> 00:17:54.700
in an intimate relationship.

412
00:17:54.783 --> 00:17:56.910
Correct. intimate.
not professional.

413
00:17:56.993 --> 00:17:58.662
Well. when you spend
100 hours a week together

414
00:17:58.745 --> 00:17:59.871
in an office building...

415
00:17:59.954 --> 00:18:01.664
No. no. no. That is ridiculous.

416
00:18:01.748 --> 00:18:03.417
If the legislature
wanted to include

417
00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:05.794
employers in the statute.
they would have done so.

418
00:18:05.919 --> 00:18:08.922
Furthermore.
there's no evidence whatsoever

419
00:18:09.005 --> 00:18:11.049
that Geller was ever
physically abusive.

420
00:18:11.132 --> 00:18:12.550
That's not true.

421
00:18:12.634 --> 00:18:14.136
Geller was constantly
threatening to harm my client.

422
00:18:14.219 --> 00:18:15.595
No. it was all hyperbole...

423
00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:17.848
Ah. a threat is a threat.

424
00:18:17.931 --> 00:18:19.349
That's a fair point.

425
00:18:19.432 --> 00:18:21.685
But the facts don't
even remotely support

426
00:18:21.768 --> 00:18:22.853
a claim of self-defense.

427
00:18:22.936 --> 00:18:24.312
The defendant followed Geller

428
00:18:24.437 --> 00:18:25.522
to the East End helipad.

429
00:18:25.605 --> 00:18:28.024
He pursued the victim.

430
00:18:28.149 --> 00:18:29.609
I understand.

431
00:18:29.693 --> 00:18:31.445
And if you're right.
and if the facts are

432
00:18:31.528 --> 00:18:34.155
as clear as you suggest.
the jury will see that.

433
00:18:34.239 --> 00:18:36.324
But Mr. Damaso is
free to present

434
00:18:36.449 --> 00:18:38.535
the defense he wants.
flaws and all.

435
00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:40.829
Your Honor.
they are inventing a narrative

436
00:18:40.954 --> 00:18:43.498
for the sole purpose
of confusing the jury.

437
00:18:43.623 --> 00:18:45.250
Pretty sure
that's Mr. Damaso's job.

438
00:18:56.261 --> 00:18:57.661
Mr. Addis.
could you please describe

439
00:18:58.013 --> 00:18:59.139
your general impression

440
00:18:59.222 --> 00:19:00.432
of the relationship
between Mr. Geller

441
00:19:00.515 --> 00:19:01.641
and the defendant?

442
00:19:01.725 --> 00:19:03.977
My general impression is.

443
00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:05.729
they really didn't have
a relationship.

444
00:19:05.812 --> 00:19:09.983
I'm not sure Jon even knew
Nick's first name.

445
00:19:10.066 --> 00:19:11.860
And what was
your general impression

446
00:19:11.943 --> 00:19:14.446
of the defendant's opinion
of Mr. Geller?

447
00:19:14.529 --> 00:19:16.364
Well. he hated him
with a passion.

448
00:19:16.489 --> 00:19:17.866
Why do you say that?

449
00:19:17.949 --> 00:19:18.951
Because he told me all the time.

450
00:19:19.034 --> 00:19:19.993
Geller drove him crazy.

451
00:19:20.076 --> 00:19:22.078
Can you be more specific?

452
00:19:22.203 --> 00:19:25.207
Geller was all about
business. money.

453
00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:26.374
Two and twenty.

454
00:19:26.458 --> 00:19:27.792
What does that mean?

455
00:19:27.917 --> 00:19:29.753
We get 2% of the assets
under management

456
00:19:29.878 --> 00:19:31.046
and 20% of the profits.

457
00:19:31.171 --> 00:19:33.006
That's all
that Geller focused on.

458
00:19:33.089 --> 00:19:36.009
growing our assets
and increasing our profits.

459
00:19:36.092 --> 00:19:39.262
He had absolutely no interest
in anything else.

460
00:19:39.387 --> 00:19:40.972
no empathy for employees.

461
00:19:41.056 --> 00:19:43.267
no interest in
people's personal lives.

462
00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:44.893
But everyone knew that.

463
00:19:44.976 --> 00:19:46.144
That was the bargain
we all made.

464
00:19:46.227 --> 00:19:48.397
Work like a dog.
be treated like crap.

465
00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:51.232
get rich by 35.

466
00:19:51.316 --> 00:19:53.193
But Nick didn't seem
to comprehend that.

467
00:19:53.276 --> 00:19:54.861
He never quite figured out

468
00:19:54.944 --> 00:19:56.446
that he'd made a deal
with the devil.

469
00:19:56.529 --> 00:19:58.031
Did the defendant ever say
that he intended

470
00:19:58.114 --> 00:20:00.158
to inflict harm upon Mr. Geller?

471
00:20:00.241 --> 00:20:01.951
-Yes.
-When was this?

472
00:20:02.077 --> 00:20:04.996
About a week or so
before Geller was killed.

473
00:20:05.080 --> 00:20:06.206
What did he say?

474
00:20:06.289 --> 00:20:08.500
He was tired of Geller
disrespecting him.

475
00:20:08.583 --> 00:20:11.127
Said if he kept it up.
he was going to beat his ass.

476
00:20:11.252 --> 00:20:14.005
To be honest.
I just thought he was venting.

477
00:20:14.089 --> 00:20:15.382
Can you please describe
what happened

478
00:20:15.465 --> 00:20:17.425
on the morning of July 19th?

479
00:20:17.509 --> 00:20:19.594
Nick and I were working.
trying to close a deal

480
00:20:19.678 --> 00:20:20.970
we'd been working on for months.

481
00:20:21.054 --> 00:20:23.056
And Geller stopped by.
carrying this new putter

482
00:20:23.139 --> 00:20:26.309
he had custom built.
and he was pissed.

483
00:20:26.393 --> 00:20:29.270
He said that we needed
to close the deal this week.

484
00:20:29.354 --> 00:20:31.148
or we'd both be fired.

485
00:20:31.231 --> 00:20:32.566
And then what happened?

486
00:20:32.649 --> 00:20:36.486
He left. said he was off
to the Hamptons.

487
00:20:36.569 --> 00:20:38.780
How did the defendant react?

488
00:20:38.863 --> 00:20:41.074
He just kept complaining
about Geller.

489
00:20:41.157 --> 00:20:42.575
And then about 15 minutes later.

490
00:20:42.701 --> 00:20:44.994
he just walked out.

491
00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:46.163
Did he tell you
where he was going?

492
00:20:46.246 --> 00:20:47.664
No.

493
00:20:47.747 --> 00:20:50.000
I just assumed that he had hit
his breaking point.

494
00:20:50.083 --> 00:20:51.293
that he quit.

495
00:20:51.376 --> 00:20:52.711
Happens a lot.

496
00:20:52.794 --> 00:20:54.045
Nothing further.

497
00:20:56.715 --> 00:20:59.426
If Mr. Rossi wanted to go home

498
00:20:59.592 --> 00:21:03.680
and take a nap
on the morning of July 19th.

499
00:21:03.763 --> 00:21:05.098
Mr. Geller would have
fired him. correct?

500
00:21:05.181 --> 00:21:06.850
Yes. absolutely.

501
00:21:06.933 --> 00:21:09.519
If Nick Rossi were ill

502
00:21:09.602 --> 00:21:12.897
and left the office
to see a doctor.

503
00:21:13.023 --> 00:21:14.566
Geller would have fired him
for that as well. correct?

504
00:21:14.649 --> 00:21:16.944
No. Geller wasn't that crazy.

505
00:21:17.027 --> 00:21:18.820
He probably would have
just tortured him

506
00:21:18.903 --> 00:21:20.989
a little bit down the road.

507
00:21:21.072 --> 00:21:24.325
Look. this business
isn't for everyone.

508
00:21:24.409 --> 00:21:26.828
But if you sign up for it.
if you're lucky enough

509
00:21:26.911 --> 00:21:28.913
to get one of the coveted
few seats of the table.

510
00:21:28.997 --> 00:21:30.499
you don't get to be soft.

511
00:21:30.582 --> 00:21:32.792
You have to be prepared
to do whatever it takes...

512
00:21:32.876 --> 00:21:34.002
working through illnesses.

513
00:21:34.085 --> 00:21:36.880
birthday dinners.
friends' weddings.

514
00:21:36.963 --> 00:21:39.966
If you don't want to do that
or you can't do that.

515
00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:41.718
then get a job
whipping up cappuccinos

516
00:21:41.801 --> 00:21:45.263
at a café in the West Village.

517
00:21:45.347 --> 00:21:47.015
Nothing further.

518
00:21:49.309 --> 00:21:51.269
There were several
lacerations to the scalp.

519
00:21:51.353 --> 00:21:55.273
including a linear laceration
measuring 13 centimeters

520
00:21:55.357 --> 00:21:59.361
on the left temporal region
and a stellate laceration

521
00:21:59.444 --> 00:22:02.656
with surrounding abrasions
on the right parietal region.

522
00:22:02.739 --> 00:22:04.574
Multiple skull fractures
were identified

523
00:22:04.699 --> 00:22:05.825
on the left frontal lobe.

524
00:22:05.992 --> 00:22:08.161
as well as evidence
of cerebral contusions

525
00:22:08.244 --> 00:22:10.288
and subarachnoid hemorrhage.

526
00:22:10.372 --> 00:22:11.623
Does the medical report state

527
00:22:11.706 --> 00:22:13.750
how many times
Mr. Geller was struck?

528
00:22:13.833 --> 00:22:15.377
Yes.

529
00:22:15.543 --> 00:22:17.963
Based on an examination
of the decedent's injuries.

530
00:22:18.046 --> 00:22:20.299
the decedent was struck
at least 10.

531
00:22:20.382 --> 00:22:23.259
possibly 11 times
with violent force.

532
00:22:23.343 --> 00:22:25.637
Is there a reason
why the medical examiner

533
00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:27.263
wasn't more exact?

534
00:22:27.389 --> 00:22:29.265
Sometimes when the violence
is so severe.

535
00:22:29.349 --> 00:22:31.267
it's just impossible
for the medical examiner

536
00:22:31.351 --> 00:22:33.269
to determine
exactly how many blows

537
00:22:33.353 --> 00:22:35.980
were administered with any
real degree of certainty.

538
00:22:36.064 --> 00:22:38.525
In other words.
this attack was so violent.

539
00:22:38.608 --> 00:22:39.568
it is impossible
for the medical...

540
00:22:39.651 --> 00:22:40.568
Objection.

541
00:22:40.652 --> 00:22:41.903
Sustained.

542
00:22:42.946 --> 00:22:44.406
Nothing further.

543
00:22:44.489 --> 00:22:46.742
No questions.

544
00:22:46.825 --> 00:22:48.326
People rest.

545
00:22:50.787 --> 00:22:52.372
Photos seemed to do the trick.

546
00:22:52.455 --> 00:22:55.167
The jury was horrified.
in a good way.

547
00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:56.710
I just need
to keep reminding them

548
00:22:56.793 --> 00:22:59.254
that Geller was the victim.
not Rossi.

549
00:22:59.337 --> 00:23:01.256
and that having a bad boss
doesn't give you

550
00:23:01.339 --> 00:23:02.674
the right to commit murder.

551
00:23:02.757 --> 00:23:04.259
Yeah.

552
00:23:04.342 --> 00:23:06.052
The problem is.
people hate bad bosses.

553
00:23:06.219 --> 00:23:08.889
and lots of people have them.
which means

554
00:23:08.972 --> 00:23:11.599
some or even most of the jury
have them.

555
00:23:11.683 --> 00:23:14.436
That may be true.
but that's a good thing too.

556
00:23:14.561 --> 00:23:15.937
Why is that?

557
00:23:16.021 --> 00:23:17.939
Well. none of the jurors
killed their bad boss.

558
00:23:19.774 --> 00:23:21.234
You didn't know my husband.
You don't know...

559
00:23:21.317 --> 00:23:22.819
And you don't know my son.

560
00:23:22.902 --> 00:23:23.945
Why didn't he just quit?

561
00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:25.364
He didn't have to bash
his face in!

562
00:23:25.447 --> 00:23:26.990
Your husband treated him
like dirt!

563
00:23:27.073 --> 00:23:28.200
Didn't let him come
to my wedding.

564
00:23:28.283 --> 00:23:29.409
My wedding!
-OK. OK.

565
00:23:29.492 --> 00:23:30.869
-That's what he...
-OK. OK. Calm down.

566
00:23:30.952 --> 00:23:31.995
-Stop talking to me!
-Screw your wedding!

567
00:23:32.078 --> 00:23:33.121
Everyone. back up. now.

568
00:23:33.204 --> 00:23:34.456
At least you have your husband!

569
00:23:36.374 --> 00:23:38.460
I know
you're just doing your job.

570
00:23:38.543 --> 00:23:40.587
but you're on the wrong end
of this one.

571
00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:42.506
A man can only be pushed so far.

572
00:23:42.589 --> 00:23:44.799
Let's go. Time to leave.

573
00:23:51.806 --> 00:23:53.141
{\an8}<i> Dr. Morley. did you have
the opportunity</i>

574
00:23:53.224 --> 00:23:54.768
to examine Mr. Rossi?

575
00:23:54.851 --> 00:23:56.019
I did.

576
00:23:56.102 --> 00:23:57.980
And what were your observations?

577
00:23:58.063 --> 00:24:00.231
That he is. in essence.
experiencing the same level

578
00:24:00.315 --> 00:24:03.568
of fear. stress.
helplessness. and anger

579
00:24:03.651 --> 00:24:07.364
that abused wives or domestic
partners tend to suffer.

580
00:24:07.447 --> 00:24:09.657
Would it be reasonable
for someone suffering

581
00:24:09.783 --> 00:24:12.244
this kind of abuse to believe

582
00:24:12.327 --> 00:24:13.704
they were in
imminent fear of death?

583
00:24:13.787 --> 00:24:14.829
Yes.

584
00:24:14.913 --> 00:24:16.581
Would it be reasonable
for someone

585
00:24:16.706 --> 00:24:20.418
suffering from this condition
to believe the only way

586
00:24:20.502 --> 00:24:22.962
to end the abusive behavior
was to kill the abuser?

587
00:24:23.046 --> 00:24:25.465
Yes. absolutely.

588
00:24:25.548 --> 00:24:27.300
Nothing further.

589
00:24:27.384 --> 00:24:30.637
There is no condition
or... or illness

590
00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:32.722
in the Diagnostic
and Statistical Manual

591
00:24:32.806 --> 00:24:34.808
of Mental Disorders called
Battered Banker Syndrome.

592
00:24:34.891 --> 00:24:36.101
Correct.

593
00:24:36.184 --> 00:24:38.353
And most cases
of Abused Person Syndrome

594
00:24:38.436 --> 00:24:41.607
are a result of abusive
intimate relationships.

595
00:24:41.690 --> 00:24:42.941
The victim typically lives with

596
00:24:43.024 --> 00:24:44.526
or is sexually involved
with the abuser.

597
00:24:44.609 --> 00:24:45.902
-Yes.
-And to be clear.

598
00:24:46.027 --> 00:24:48.029
in your examination
of the defendant.

599
00:24:48.113 --> 00:24:50.657
he never once mentioned
that... that he was

600
00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:52.576
intimately or sexually involved
with Mr. Geller.

601
00:24:52.659 --> 00:24:53.785
He did not.

602
00:24:53.868 --> 00:24:54.953
He didn't live with Mr. Geller?

603
00:24:55.036 --> 00:24:56.037
That's correct.

604
00:24:56.162 --> 00:24:57.497
And Mr. Geller never

605
00:24:57.580 --> 00:24:59.499
put his hands
on the defendant. either.

606
00:24:59.582 --> 00:25:00.917
No.

607
00:25:01.001 --> 00:25:02.460
OK. Thank you.

608
00:25:05.547 --> 00:25:07.924
On the morning of July 19th.
did you hear Jon Geller

609
00:25:08.008 --> 00:25:09.592
arguing with Nick Rossi?

610
00:25:09.676 --> 00:25:10.719
Yes.

611
00:25:10.844 --> 00:25:13.221
What was the basis
of the argument?

612
00:25:13.304 --> 00:25:15.974
Geller was pissed the McKenna
deal hadn't closed yet.

613
00:25:16.057 --> 00:25:18.059
He was worried
the interest rate might spike

614
00:25:18.143 --> 00:25:19.853
and kill the projected IRR.

615
00:25:19.936 --> 00:25:22.272
Meaning what?
He'd make less money?

616
00:25:22.355 --> 00:25:23.690
Yes.

617
00:25:23.773 --> 00:25:25.484
Did you hear the exact words
Mr. Geller said

618
00:25:25.567 --> 00:25:27.318
to Mr. Rossi?
-Yes.

619
00:25:27.402 --> 00:25:29.988
I was standing in the hallway
near the coffee room.

620
00:25:30.071 --> 00:25:32.950
And I heard Geller
screaming at Nick.

621
00:25:33.033 --> 00:25:34.743
He said something like.

622
00:25:34.826 --> 00:25:38.538
"Do not leave this office
until the deal is closed.

623
00:25:38.621 --> 00:25:41.332
"If you so much as take
a walk around the block.

624
00:25:41.416 --> 00:25:42.584
"I will fire you.

625
00:25:42.667 --> 00:25:44.211
"No. wait. That's too kind.

626
00:25:44.294 --> 00:25:47.130
"I will literally beat you
with a baseball bat

627
00:25:47.213 --> 00:25:49.508
and watch you bleed to death."

628
00:25:49.591 --> 00:25:52.886
Have you seen
or heard Mr. Geller

629
00:25:52.969 --> 00:25:56.640
verbally attack Nick Rossi
before that incident?

630
00:25:56.723 --> 00:25:58.391
Yes. several times.

631
00:25:58.475 --> 00:25:59.934
How would you characterize

632
00:26:00.018 --> 00:26:02.103
the nature
of these verbal attacks?

633
00:26:02.228 --> 00:26:05.691
As sadistic.

634
00:26:05.774 --> 00:26:09.236
I think Geller enjoyed
berating Nick.

635
00:26:09.319 --> 00:26:11.071
I once heard him say.
"You are probably

636
00:26:11.154 --> 00:26:14.574
the dumbest associate
I've ever hired."

637
00:26:14.657 --> 00:26:16.659
Can you recall
any other verbal abuse?

638
00:26:16.785 --> 00:26:20.163
About a month ago.
Geller was really upset

639
00:26:20.246 --> 00:26:22.874
and started attacking
Nick's Italian heritage.

640
00:26:22.999 --> 00:26:27.087
It was so outrageous.
I just started recording.

641
00:26:27.170 --> 00:26:28.839
Your Honor. I'd like
to introduce this video

642
00:26:28.922 --> 00:26:31.633
as Defense Exhibit 22.

643
00:26:33.426 --> 00:26:34.886
{\an8}<i> I can't believe
I actually hired you.</i>

644
00:26:34.969 --> 00:26:36.346
{\an8}<i>I don't know
what I was thinking.</i>

645
00:26:36.429 --> 00:26:37.681
{\an8}<i>And I swear to God.</i>

646
00:26:37.764 --> 00:26:39.307
{\an8}<i>if you don't somehow</i>

647
00:26:39.432 --> 00:26:41.726
{\an8}<i>get smarter. a lot smarter.</i>

648
00:26:41.810 --> 00:26:43.770
{\an8}<i>I'm gonna throw your dumb ass
out on the street.</i>

649
00:26:43.895 --> 00:26:45.230
{\an8}<i>and you're going to be
pouring concrete</i>

650
00:26:45.313 --> 00:26:47.107
{\an8}<i>with your paisans from Brooklyn.</i>

651
00:26:47.190 --> 00:26:48.609
{\an8}<i>-I'm sorry. I'll do better.
-No. no. no.</i>

652
00:26:48.692 --> 00:26:50.527
{\an8}<i>You don't get to apologize
or make mistakes.</i>

653
00:26:50.610 --> 00:26:51.778
{\an8}<i>That's not how it works.</i>

654
00:26:51.861 --> 00:26:53.905
{\an8}<i>You either get it right.
or get out!</i>

655
00:26:53.988 --> 00:26:55.699
{\an8}<i>And I'm serious.</i>

656
00:26:55.782 --> 00:26:58.076
{\an8}<i>You screw up one more time.</i>

657
00:26:58.159 --> 00:26:59.869
{\an8}<i>you're gone...</i>

658
00:26:59.953 --> 00:27:02.372
{\an8}<i>from here. from Wall Street.</i>

659
00:27:02.455 --> 00:27:05.750
{\an8}<i>Oh. I'll blow your ass up
at every firm in this city.</i>

660
00:27:09.754 --> 00:27:11.881
So what happened
after the video ended?

661
00:27:11.965 --> 00:27:14.467
Geller walked back to his
office like nothing happened.

662
00:27:14.551 --> 00:27:16.511
In fact. he was kind of smiling.

663
00:27:16.636 --> 00:27:20.390
like he was proud of his rant.

664
00:27:20.473 --> 00:27:23.059
And what about Nick?

665
00:27:23.143 --> 00:27:25.062
Nick started crying.

666
00:27:25.145 --> 00:27:26.938
It was difficult to watch.

667
00:27:27.022 --> 00:27:28.898
Nothing further.

668
00:27:32.277 --> 00:27:33.403
No questions.

669
00:27:33.528 --> 00:27:34.947
The witness is excused.

670
00:27:35.030 --> 00:27:37.449
The defense calls Ron Moffit.

671
00:27:37.532 --> 00:27:39.325
Your Honor.
can we meet in chambers?

672
00:27:39.409 --> 00:27:41.828
There is nothing
Mr. Moffit can testify to

673
00:27:41.911 --> 00:27:44.956
that is remotely relevant
to this case.

674
00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:46.916
Really?

675
00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:49.252
You don't think the fact
that Mr. Moffit's son

676
00:27:49.377 --> 00:27:51.421
died at his desk
while working for Geller

677
00:27:51.504 --> 00:27:52.798
is relevant
to this current case?

678
00:27:52.881 --> 00:27:54.883
It literally couldn't be
more relevant.

679
00:27:54.966 --> 00:27:56.343
It establishes
the reasonableness

680
00:27:56.426 --> 00:27:59.012
of Mr. Rossi's fear
of imminent death.

681
00:27:59.095 --> 00:28:01.598
Can you be more specific?

682
00:28:01.723 --> 00:28:03.767
Geller abused
this young man. Ted Moffit.

683
00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:05.518
He abused Rossi the same way.

684
00:28:05.602 --> 00:28:07.937
And that kid wound up dead...

685
00:28:08.063 --> 00:28:10.815
died at his desk
after working 72 hours

686
00:28:10.899 --> 00:28:13.151
because Geller refused
to let him leave.

687
00:28:14.694 --> 00:28:17.030
Sounds relevant to me.

688
00:28:17.113 --> 00:28:18.740
Call your witness.

689
00:28:22.285 --> 00:28:26.206
When was the last time
you talked to your son?

690
00:28:26.289 --> 00:28:27.957
The day before he passed.

691
00:28:28.041 --> 00:28:29.459
Was he sick?

692
00:28:29.584 --> 00:28:31.961
Yeah. he had the flu.

693
00:28:32.045 --> 00:28:34.506
I told him to stay home
and get some rest.

694
00:28:34.589 --> 00:28:38.551
He said that was not an option.

695
00:28:38.635 --> 00:28:40.470
What do you mean by that?

696
00:28:40.553 --> 00:28:43.306
Ted said they were working
on some important transaction.

697
00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:45.308
and he needed to be
in the office.

698
00:28:45.392 --> 00:28:48.061
I said. I don't care
how big a damn deal that was.

699
00:28:48.144 --> 00:28:50.814
You need to stay home
and get in bed.

700
00:28:50.939 --> 00:28:53.024
And how did he respond?

701
00:28:53.108 --> 00:28:56.444
That if he stayed home.
Geller would have him fired.

702
00:28:56.569 --> 00:29:00.949
So he went to the office.

703
00:29:01.032 --> 00:29:03.118
He worked all night.

704
00:29:03.201 --> 00:29:06.788
and he passed the next night
at his desk.

705
00:29:06.871 --> 00:29:07.997
Thank you.

706
00:29:08.081 --> 00:29:09.457
Nothing further.

707
00:29:14.921 --> 00:29:17.257
Mr. Moffit.

708
00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:20.051
Mr. Geller didn't force your son

709
00:29:20.135 --> 00:29:22.137
to go to work that day.

710
00:29:22.220 --> 00:29:25.140
If Ted had listened
to your advice

711
00:29:25.223 --> 00:29:26.933
and stayed at home.

712
00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:29.686
no one from Higson Capital
would have come

713
00:29:29.769 --> 00:29:31.313
to his apartment
and dragged him to the office.

714
00:29:31.396 --> 00:29:33.023
No. of course not.

715
00:29:33.106 --> 00:29:34.774
But he would have been fired.

716
00:29:34.899 --> 00:29:36.860
According to Ted.

717
00:29:36.943 --> 00:29:38.445
He may have believed
that he would have been fired.

718
00:29:38.528 --> 00:29:40.739
but there's no evidence of that.

719
00:29:40.822 --> 00:29:42.741
That was simply
Ted's impression.

720
00:29:42.824 --> 00:29:45.285
All I can say
is that Ted didn't think

721
00:29:45.368 --> 00:29:48.664
he could stay home that day.
even with the flu.

722
00:29:48.747 --> 00:29:52.876
Ted also had a pre-existing
heart condition as well.

723
00:29:52.959 --> 00:29:55.253
arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy.

724
00:29:55.337 --> 00:29:56.671
That's correct.

725
00:29:56.755 --> 00:29:59.299
An autopsy was conducted
following Ted's death?

726
00:29:59.382 --> 00:30:00.592
Yes.

727
00:30:00.675 --> 00:30:01.927
And the official
cause of death was

728
00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:03.887
determined to be
sudden cardiac arrest.

729
00:30:03.970 --> 00:30:05.305
Yes.

730
00:30:05.388 --> 00:30:08.683
So from a medical perspective.

731
00:30:08.767 --> 00:30:10.352
Mr. Geller had
absolutely nothing

732
00:30:10.435 --> 00:30:13.396
to do with your son's death.

733
00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:15.274
What?

734
00:30:15.357 --> 00:30:18.068
Well. he didn't
choke him to death.

735
00:30:18.151 --> 00:30:21.446
He didn't stick
a damn knife in his chest.

736
00:30:21.529 --> 00:30:25.533
But Geller contributed
to his death.

737
00:30:25.617 --> 00:30:29.287
All that stress.
all that pressure.

738
00:30:29.371 --> 00:30:33.041
plus the lack of sleep.

739
00:30:33.124 --> 00:30:35.669
that is what caused
Ted's heart to malfunction.

740
00:30:35.752 --> 00:30:37.921
That's your belief. sir.

741
00:30:38.004 --> 00:30:40.840
But the medical examiner
did not find any evidence...

742
00:30:40.924 --> 00:30:43.260
I don't care about
the damn medical examiner.

743
00:30:43.343 --> 00:30:48.056
If it weren't for Jon Geller.
my son would still be alive.

744
00:30:48.139 --> 00:30:50.100
I guarantee that.

745
00:30:53.895 --> 00:30:55.480
Nothing further.

746
00:31:07.075 --> 00:31:08.715
Can you please tell the jury
what happened

747
00:31:09.119 --> 00:31:11.788
on the morning of July 19th?

748
00:31:11.871 --> 00:31:15.458
I was working on this deal
day after day.

749
00:31:15.542 --> 00:31:17.044
trying to...
we were trying to buy

750
00:31:17.127 --> 00:31:19.546
this luxury goods company
called McKenna.

751
00:31:19.629 --> 00:31:21.423
When you say day after day.
what do you mean?

752
00:31:21.548 --> 00:31:22.799
It means
I didn't leave the office

753
00:31:22.882 --> 00:31:24.759
for three straight days.

754
00:31:24.843 --> 00:31:26.970
Not even at night?

755
00:31:27.053 --> 00:31:28.138
No.

756
00:31:28.221 --> 00:31:30.640
I slept in my office.

757
00:31:30.724 --> 00:31:33.601
Can you describe
your... your state of mind?

758
00:31:33.685 --> 00:31:37.189
I felt like I was trapped.

759
00:31:37.272 --> 00:31:39.858
like I couldn't leave
the building.

760
00:31:39.983 --> 00:31:42.611
I literally felt like
I was gonna die like Ted.

761
00:31:42.694 --> 00:31:44.446
And that scared me.

762
00:31:46.614 --> 00:31:48.950
I was the one
who found Ted's body.

763
00:31:49.075 --> 00:31:50.953
At first.
I thought he was sleeping.

764
00:31:51.036 --> 00:31:54.456
but he was dead.

765
00:31:54.539 --> 00:32:00.003
If you felt this way.
why didn't you just quit?

766
00:32:00.086 --> 00:32:01.963
I thought about it all the time.

767
00:32:02.047 --> 00:32:04.049
but I couldn't do it.

768
00:32:04.132 --> 00:32:07.177
I needed to stick it out. succeed.

769
00:32:07.260 --> 00:32:09.721
Even though Geller was verbally

770
00:32:09.804 --> 00:32:11.139
and mentally abusing you.

771
00:32:11.222 --> 00:32:12.557
-Objection.
-Sustained.

772
00:32:12.640 --> 00:32:13.933
I'll rephrase...

773
00:32:14.017 --> 00:32:17.687
even though you were miserable
and felt trapped.

774
00:32:17.771 --> 00:32:18.938
Yes.

775
00:32:19.022 --> 00:32:20.690
Look. I know
it doesn't make any sense.

776
00:32:20.774 --> 00:32:23.985
but I didn't think
quitting was an option.

777
00:32:24.110 --> 00:32:25.779
Not for a guy like me. anyway.

778
00:32:25.862 --> 00:32:26.696
What do you mean by that?

779
00:32:26.821 --> 00:32:28.323
I didn't go to Harvard.

780
00:32:28.406 --> 00:32:31.159
I don't have an MBA.

781
00:32:31.242 --> 00:32:34.329
I kind of snuck in through
the back door. you know?

782
00:32:34.412 --> 00:32:38.667
I wanted to prove myself
and prove that I belonged.

783
00:32:38.750 --> 00:32:40.669
If I quit.
my career would be over.

784
00:32:40.752 --> 00:32:42.671
Other firms would think
that I couldn't hack it

785
00:32:42.796 --> 00:32:45.548
or that I got pushed out.

786
00:32:45.632 --> 00:32:49.970
Rich kids with perfect résumés
get to quit.

787
00:32:50.053 --> 00:32:52.639
Blue collar kids
who went to Fordham don't.

788
00:32:52.722 --> 00:32:57.394
So I sucked it up.
no matter how bad it got.

789
00:32:57.477 --> 00:32:59.729
When you left the office

790
00:32:59.854 --> 00:33:03.692
the morning of July 19th.

791
00:33:03.817 --> 00:33:05.985
what were you trying
to accomplish?

792
00:33:06.111 --> 00:33:07.445
What do you mean?

793
00:33:07.529 --> 00:33:10.532
What was your objective.
your goal?

794
00:33:10.615 --> 00:33:14.494
My goal was to free myself
of Geller's choke hold.

795
00:33:14.577 --> 00:33:17.456
Can you be more specific?

796
00:33:17.539 --> 00:33:19.541
I felt like
he was suffocating me.

797
00:33:19.624 --> 00:33:21.043
I couldn't breathe.

798
00:33:21.126 --> 00:33:23.545
I couldn't think.

799
00:33:23.628 --> 00:33:26.172
My heart was racing.

800
00:33:26.256 --> 00:33:28.842
Like I said.
I thought I was gonna die.

801
00:33:28.925 --> 00:33:31.886
So that morning.
when I left the office.

802
00:33:31.970 --> 00:33:34.514
my purpose was
to kill Jon Geller

803
00:33:34.597 --> 00:33:36.057
because I knew that if I didn't.

804
00:33:36.141 --> 00:33:38.018
I would have ended up dead.

805
00:33:41.396 --> 00:33:42.897
Your Honor. it's almost 5:00.

806
00:33:42.981 --> 00:33:44.315
Can we adjourn for the day?

807
00:33:44.399 --> 00:33:46.151
Yes. good idea.

808
00:33:46.234 --> 00:33:47.594
We'll see you all
tomorrow morning.

809
00:33:53.199 --> 00:33:56.494
So you want me to testify
as a rebuttal witness.

810
00:33:56.578 --> 00:33:59.081
I need you to help establish
Rossi's state of mind.

811
00:33:59.164 --> 00:34:01.541
his general demeanor
on the morning of the murder.

812
00:34:01.624 --> 00:34:03.293
Why?

813
00:34:03.376 --> 00:34:05.128
Because Rossi just told
the jury that he felt like

814
00:34:05.211 --> 00:34:06.797
he was on the verge of death
that morning.

815
00:34:06.880 --> 00:34:08.798
He couldn't breathe.
His heart was racing.

816
00:34:08.882 --> 00:34:11.217
And he was very convincing too.

817
00:34:11.384 --> 00:34:14.012
Yeah. well.
I'm not sure I can do that.

818
00:34:15.221 --> 00:34:16.848
Well. what do you mean?

819
00:34:16.931 --> 00:34:18.600
You were there.
You were with Rossi.

820
00:34:18.683 --> 00:34:21.019
Oh. yes.
but I've had time to reflect.

821
00:34:21.102 --> 00:34:22.979
And I'm starting to relate more

822
00:34:23.063 --> 00:34:25.523
to Rossi's point of view
on all this.

823
00:34:26.483 --> 00:34:27.942
What the hell
are you talking about?

824
00:34:28.026 --> 00:34:29.444
Jon Geller and his number two.

825
00:34:29.527 --> 00:34:31.029
Carter Getzelman.
created a hostile

826
00:34:31.112 --> 00:34:32.447
and poisonous work environment.

827
00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:34.324
They constantly
and systematically

828
00:34:34.449 --> 00:34:37.035
degraded employees.
made unreasonable

829
00:34:37.118 --> 00:34:39.913
and unhealthy demands
of their time.

830
00:34:39.996 --> 00:34:42.124
I don't wanna say anything
on the record

831
00:34:42.207 --> 00:34:45.377
that might appear to support
this type of behavior.

832
00:34:48.254 --> 00:34:50.590
You son of a bitch.

833
00:34:50.715 --> 00:34:52.050
Excuse me?

834
00:34:52.133 --> 00:34:53.968
You're using this trial

835
00:34:54.052 --> 00:34:57.013
to take out the new CEO.
Getzelman.

836
00:34:57.097 --> 00:34:58.223
No.

837
00:34:58.306 --> 00:34:59.433
I'm trying to do
the right thing.

838
00:34:59.516 --> 00:35:00.975
Uh. no.

839
00:35:01.059 --> 00:35:02.435
No. no. no.

840
00:35:02.602 --> 00:35:06.565
No. the right thing
is to tell the truth.

841
00:35:06.648 --> 00:35:08.692
is to get justice
for Jon Geller.

842
00:35:08.775 --> 00:35:12.821
not to exploit his murder
for personal gain.

843
00:35:12.904 --> 00:35:16.658
You said that you liked him.
that you respected...

844
00:35:16.783 --> 00:35:18.201
that he made you rich.

845
00:35:18.284 --> 00:35:20.704
He did.

846
00:35:20.787 --> 00:35:23.164
Just not rich enough.

847
00:35:28.169 --> 00:35:31.464
Hope it's worth it.

848
00:35:32.799 --> 00:35:34.593
I knew Geller was
a shark. but...

849
00:35:34.676 --> 00:35:36.678
It's all just a game
to these guys.

850
00:35:36.761 --> 00:35:40.223
They see a pathway to more
money or power. they take it.

851
00:35:40.306 --> 00:35:42.225
Hell of a way to live.

852
00:35:42.308 --> 00:35:44.019
if you exclude
the Hamptons houses

853
00:35:44.144 --> 00:35:46.646
and the private jets.

854
00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:48.898
I need to make it clear on cross

855
00:35:49.024 --> 00:35:52.610
that Geller's so-called threats
were hollow.

856
00:35:52.694 --> 00:35:55.780
They were just bluster
and hyperbole.

857
00:35:55.864 --> 00:35:58.533
and that no matter how
disrespectful Geller could be

858
00:35:58.616 --> 00:36:01.453
or how helpless Rossi felt.

859
00:36:01.536 --> 00:36:03.621
it didn't justify murder.

860
00:36:03.705 --> 00:36:05.999
The simple truth is.
Rossi got tired

861
00:36:06.082 --> 00:36:10.254
of Geller degrading him.
and he snapped.

862
00:36:10.337 --> 00:36:12.088
If that's what you believe.

863
00:36:12.172 --> 00:36:14.841
shouldn't we be considering
a lesser charge?

864
00:36:14.966 --> 00:36:16.009
Excuse me?

865
00:36:16.092 --> 00:36:17.510
You just said he snapped.

866
00:36:17.594 --> 00:36:18.887
If that's what you think.
then...

867
00:36:19.012 --> 00:36:20.388
Yeah. yeah. he snapped.

868
00:36:20.472 --> 00:36:23.600
but it was
a cold-blooded attack.

869
00:36:23.683 --> 00:36:26.394
based on... on rage. anger. hate.

870
00:36:26.561 --> 00:36:30.565
Yeah. yeah. sure. but he was
in a tough situation.

871
00:36:30.648 --> 00:36:32.150
What do you mean?

872
00:36:32.233 --> 00:36:33.568
He knew he didn't fit in. right?

873
00:36:33.651 --> 00:36:35.570
That he was different than most.

874
00:36:35.653 --> 00:36:37.781
if not all.
the other associates.

875
00:36:37.906 --> 00:36:40.533
Pretty sure they call that
imposter syndrome.

876
00:36:41.951 --> 00:36:43.161
I get it.

877
00:36:43.244 --> 00:36:46.498
And it's understandable
for someone

878
00:36:46.581 --> 00:36:50.627
to have doubts
about his or her status

879
00:36:50.710 --> 00:36:52.128
or sense of belonging

880
00:36:52.212 --> 00:36:54.047
within a highly competitive
organization.

881
00:36:54.130 --> 00:36:55.924
but it obviously doesn't
justify murder.

882
00:36:56.049 --> 00:36:58.593
Oh. no. No. of course not.

883
00:36:58.677 --> 00:37:00.762
I'm just saying
it explains the...

884
00:37:00.887 --> 00:37:03.807
the anger and stress.

885
00:37:03.890 --> 00:37:05.642
You know. it's not easy
to work at a place

886
00:37:05.725 --> 00:37:08.770
filled with really smart.
really driven people

887
00:37:08.853 --> 00:37:10.022
who all went to the best schools

888
00:37:10.105 --> 00:37:12.857
when you're just a regular Joe.

889
00:37:12.941 --> 00:37:18.655
You know. you try really hard
to fit in and be one of them.

890
00:37:18.738 --> 00:37:22.033
But deep down.
you know you're not.

891
00:37:25.412 --> 00:37:28.790
I understand
how difficult that might be

892
00:37:28.873 --> 00:37:34.087
and that it's easy
for someone in that situation

893
00:37:34.170 --> 00:37:38.299
to feel insecure at times.

894
00:37:38.466 --> 00:37:42.220
But once again. it has
nothing to do with this case.

895
00:37:42.303 --> 00:37:45.765
It doesn't matter that
Rossi seems like a decent guy

896
00:37:45.849 --> 00:37:47.809
and that he was
trying hard to fit in

897
00:37:47.892 --> 00:37:51.647
or that he was being
overworked and disrespected.

898
00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:52.856
That's the job.

899
00:37:52.939 --> 00:37:55.859
That's what he signed up for.

900
00:37:55.942 --> 00:37:58.278
Well. what can I say?

901
00:37:58.361 --> 00:38:00.530
I like to root for the underdog.

902
00:38:00.613 --> 00:38:02.115
I know.

903
00:38:02.198 --> 00:38:05.201
But unfortunately.
prosecuting decent people

904
00:38:05.285 --> 00:38:08.872
who do horrible things.
people like Rossi.

905
00:38:08.955 --> 00:38:10.207
that's part of our job.

906
00:38:10.290 --> 00:38:12.334
That is what we signed up for.

907
00:38:17.505 --> 00:38:20.216
{\an8}When you accepted the offer
to join Higson Capital.

908
00:38:20.342 --> 00:38:21.926
you knew that you'd be
required to work

909
00:38:22.010 --> 00:38:23.720
incredibly long hours. correct?

910
00:38:23.803 --> 00:38:25.096
Yes.

911
00:38:25.180 --> 00:38:27.140
And that you would be asked
to work weekends

912
00:38:27.223 --> 00:38:29.059
and even through the night.
on occasion?

913
00:38:29.142 --> 00:38:30.394
Yes.

914
00:38:30.477 --> 00:38:31.936
But that wasn't the problem.

915
00:38:33.146 --> 00:38:34.439
I don't know what you mean.

916
00:38:34.522 --> 00:38:37.859
Well. the problem was
Jon Geller.

917
00:38:37.942 --> 00:38:38.902
You despised him.

918
00:38:38.985 --> 00:38:41.404
You thought he was an elitist?

919
00:38:41.488 --> 00:38:43.198
Yes to both questions.

920
00:38:43.281 --> 00:38:46.451
It's hard

921
00:38:46.534 --> 00:38:49.371
to work at a place like Higson.

922
00:38:49.454 --> 00:38:51.873
a place that is filled
with really smart.

923
00:38:51.956 --> 00:38:54.292
really driven people who all
went to the best schools

924
00:38:54.376 --> 00:38:56.670
when you're just a regular Joe.

925
00:38:56.753 --> 00:38:57.796
It is.

926
00:38:57.879 --> 00:38:59.214
You try really hard to fit in

927
00:38:59.381 --> 00:39:02.008
and to be one of them.
but you knew that you weren't.

928
00:39:02.092 --> 00:39:04.761
And you knew
the second you screwed up.

929
00:39:04.844 --> 00:39:06.846
Geller was going to kick
your ass to the curb.

930
00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:08.306
I suppose.

931
00:39:08.390 --> 00:39:11.309
And you hated Geller
for making you feel that way.

932
00:39:11.393 --> 00:39:12.769
That's not true.

933
00:39:12.852 --> 00:39:16.315
You didn't suffer
from Battered Banker Syndrome.

934
00:39:16.398 --> 00:39:19.484
You suffered
from imposter syndrome.

935
00:39:19.567 --> 00:39:21.194
I don't know what that means.

936
00:39:21.277 --> 00:39:24.280
It means
you knew you didn't belong.

937
00:39:24.364 --> 00:39:26.783
You knew your peers were
more accomplished than you.

938
00:39:26.866 --> 00:39:28.743
and you knew that Geller knew.

939
00:39:28.827 --> 00:39:30.621
Is there a question here?

940
00:39:30.704 --> 00:39:31.871
Get to the point. Mr. Price.

941
00:39:31.955 --> 00:39:33.039
You didn't kill Jon Geller

942
00:39:33.123 --> 00:39:34.624
because you were afraid
for your life.

943
00:39:34.708 --> 00:39:36.084
You killed Jon Geller
because he made you feel

944
00:39:36.167 --> 00:39:38.294
inadequate. small. weak.

945
00:39:38.420 --> 00:39:40.922
You resented his wealth.
his pedigree.

946
00:39:41.006 --> 00:39:42.174
I resented his arrogance.

947
00:39:42.257 --> 00:39:43.634
And that's why
you killed him. right?

948
00:39:43.717 --> 00:39:44.926
Because he was arrogant.

949
00:39:45.010 --> 00:39:46.553
Because he saw
right through your façade.

950
00:39:46.636 --> 00:39:47.804
He thought he was a king.

951
00:39:47.929 --> 00:39:49.389
that he could say
whatever he wanted.

952
00:39:49.472 --> 00:39:50.849
And that pissed you off!

953
00:39:50.932 --> 00:39:52.225
-Yes!
-Your Honor. a brief recess.

954
00:39:52.308 --> 00:39:53.852
-So you killed...
-Your Honor...

955
00:39:53.935 --> 00:39:55.646
You didn't need
to kill Jon Geller.

956
00:39:55.729 --> 00:39:56.980
You wanted to kill him.

957
00:39:57.063 --> 00:39:58.398
You're damn right I did!

958
00:39:58.481 --> 00:40:01.026
And that son of a bitch
got what he deserved!

959
00:40:25.550 --> 00:40:27.177
That might have been
the quickest

960
00:40:27.260 --> 00:40:29.095
jury deliberation ever.

961
00:40:29.179 --> 00:40:31.181
He confessed on the stand.

962
00:40:31.264 --> 00:40:32.807
Yeah.

963
00:40:32.891 --> 00:40:35.685
I still feel bad for him. though.

964
00:40:35.769 --> 00:40:37.354
Not me.

965
00:40:37.437 --> 00:40:38.563
Really?

966
00:40:38.646 --> 00:40:39.981
Not even a little?

967
00:40:41.941 --> 00:40:46.780
Well. I aspire to be
as coldhearted as you someday.

968
00:40:46.863 --> 00:40:48.073
Good luck.

969
00:40:49.449 --> 00:40:53.328
Look. I know
you were just venting earlier.

970
00:40:54.788 --> 00:40:57.332
But you are no impostor. Sam.

971
00:40:57.415 --> 00:40:58.542
And if I make you
feel that way...

972
00:40:58.625 --> 00:40:59.835
No.

973
00:40:59.918 --> 00:41:02.879
No. you don't.

974
00:41:02.962 --> 00:41:08.093
It's just. uh. the way
I see the world. I suppose.
